# 3. DESIGN SYSTEM AND VISUAL DIRECTION

## 3.1 Essential design philosophy

The entire website must be:

- Simple
- Professional
- Clean
- Elegant
- Modern but timeless
- Credible
- Visually balanced
- Readable
- Mobile-friendly
- Human-designed in appearance

**Avoid the typical AI-generated website aesthetic.**

Specifically, do not create a website with excessive gradients, random glowing objects, decorative floating orbs, glassmorphism everywhere, oversized rounded cards, unnecessary pills, scattered illustrations, or repeated three-column feature cards.

Avoid exaggerated animations, enormous typography that breaks sentences unnaturally, and sections that look like unrelated template components stitched together.

The website should resemble a thoughtfully art-directed corporate website or a premium creative agency's digital presence.

Its sophistication should come from typography, spacing, composition, authentic imagery, alignment, and strong editorial hierarchy.

## 3.2 Brand colors

Use the company's supplied PDF profile and original logo as the primary reference.

The PDF visibly contains navy blue, pale blue, cyan, red, and purple.

Until formal brand HEX codes are available, use the following approximate starting palette:

| Color | Suggested HEX | Application |
|---|---|---|
| Deep Navy | #051449 | Primary brand, headings, footer |
| White | #FFFFFF | Main page backgrounds |
| Pale Blue | #CDEEF8 | Light section backgrounds |
| Cyan | #12A9DB | Secondary emphasis, links |
| Red | #E32938 | Limited brand accent |
| Purple | #51217C | Minimal decorative/supporting use |
| Charcoal | #242A35 | Body text |
| Light Neutral | #F6F8FA | Alternate backgrounds |
| Muted Grey | #667085 | Secondary text |

Use navy and white as the dominant visual combination.

Do not use all the brand colors in every section.

Avoid rainbow gradients and high-saturation backgrounds.

Red should appear only in carefully selected brand accents, not across every call-to-action element.

Ensure accessible color contrast.

## 3.3 Typography

Use one professional sans-serif font family, preferably:

- Inter
- DM Sans
- Manrope

Choose one primary family and use it consistently.

Use restrained typography:

- Desktop H1: approximately 48–64px.
- H2: 34–46px.
- H3: 22–28px.
- Body: 16–18px.
- Small labels: 12–14px.

Adjust these responsively.

Use a clear font-weight hierarchy rather than excessive font-size changes.

Body copy must have comfortable line height.

Avoid excessive all-uppercase headings.

Avoid forced line breaks in running text.

Use naturally wrapping lines, with deliberate editorial line breaks only when truly useful.

## 3.4 Layout standards

Use a maximum content width of approximately 1200–1280px, with generous side margins.

Implement a consistent spacing system.

Maintain aligned section headings, images, cards and content.

Use alternating layouts where appropriate:

- Image left, copy right.
- Copy left, image right.
- Full-width editorial image.
- Simple service grids.
- Minimal information lists.
- Clean text-first sections.

Avoid repeating the same layout on every page.

Buttons should be rectangular or gently rounded, not exaggerated pill shapes.

Use subtle borders rather than excessive shadows.

## 3.5 Photography and visual assets

Use relevant, credible photography.

Images should include:

- Technology professionals working.
- Website and application development environments.
- Photography and videography production setups.
- Branding and print materials.
- Professionals collaborating.
- Realistic business environments.
- Corporate conferences and events.
- Educational or creative communities.
- Product photography for the marketplace.

Use authentic photography wherever possible.

Do not use illustrations of robots, abstract AI brains, excessive 3D floating shapes, fake dashboards, or stereotypical AI images.

If genuine company photographs are unavailable, use carefully selected temporary photography.

Mark placeholder assets clearly in the code or CMS.

Do not fabricate BTMT staff photos, client logos, completed projects, awards or testimonials.

## 3.6 Motion and interaction

Animation must be subtle and functional.

Allowed:

- Gentle section reveal transitions.
- Smooth dropdown interactions.
- Subtle button hover states.
- Small image hover transitions.
- Smooth mobile navigation.
- Accordion expansion.
- Loading indicators where needed.

Avoid:

- Scroll hijacking.
- Parallax on every section.
- Animated gradient backgrounds.
- Heavy page transition effects.
- Constant floating elements.
- Staggered animations on every piece of text.

Respect reduced-motion settings.

